---
import Sub from '../layouts/Sub.astro';
import { servicePageSchema } from '../data/site.js';

const description =
  'Pool deck sealing in St. George, Utah with Salt Shield 303 — a penetrating sealer that stops salt crystallisation without changing the slip resistance of the deck. Free on-site assessment.';

const schema = servicePageSchema({
  path: '/pool-deck-sealing',
  name: 'Pool deck sealing and salt protection',
  description,
  crumb: 'Pool deck sealing',
});
---

<Sub
  title="Pool Deck Sealing & Salt Protection | St. George | Armour Crete"
  description={description}
  crumb="Pool deck sealing"
  schema={schema}
>
  <Fragment slot="h1">Salt is eating<br />your pool deck.</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    Salt-water pools are everywhere in southern Utah, and so is the damage they
    leave on the concrete around them. We seal pool decks with a penetrating
    product built for exactly this — and it does not change how the deck feels
    under wet, bare feet.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="aside">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>0</dt><dd>Change to skid or slip resistance — manufacturer stated</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Clear</dt><dd>No film, no residue, no shine unless you ask for it</dd></div>
      <div><dt>24h</dt><dd>Cure before the deck is back in use</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Free</dt><dd>On-site assessment, no obligation</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why pool decks fail</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">The damage happens<br />inside the pore.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose rv">
        <p>
          Salt dissolves in splash-out and spray, soaks into the pores of the
          concrete, and then the desert does the rest: the water evaporates, the
          salt crystallises, and crystals growing inside a confined pore push hard
          enough to lift the surface right off the slab. That is the flaking and
          pitting you see in a ring around older pools — it is not wear, it is
          crystal pressure from the inside.
        </p>
        <p>
          Water-softener discharge does the same thing to whatever slab it drains
          onto, which is why we look at more than the pool surround when we come
          out.
        </p>
        <p>
          The fix is to keep the saltwater out of the pore network in the first
          place. A penetrating sealer blocks the water and the chlorides riding in
          it, without laying anything on the surface.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec sec-dark">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-lt">What goes on it</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Salt Shield 303<br />exists for this.</h2>
        <a class="plink" href="/products">What each Deco product does →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <ul class="dotlist">
          <li>Clear silane/siloxane emulsion that penetrates deep into the slab</li>
          <li>Does not change the skid or slip resistance of the concrete</li>
          <li>Reduces spalling, freeze–thaw damage, dusting and mildew</li>
          <li>Non-hazardous, non-toxic, non-flammable</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note" style="border-color: rgba(244,240,233,0.3); color: rgba(244,240,233,0.6);">
          It is a penetrating sealer, not a coating — so it does not make a wet
          deck slicker than it already was. High-shine finishes are a different
          conversation, because those do leave a layer; if you want a
          <a class="tlink" href="/wet-look-concrete-sealing">wet look</a> somewhere
          that gets walked on barefoot and wet, say so at the assessment and we
          will talk it through rather than just quoting it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">Travertine &amp; stone surrounds</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Not all decks<br />are concrete.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose rv">
        <p>
          Travertine is everywhere around southern Utah pools, and it stains in
          ways that do not come back out. Stone that will not absorb a penetrating
          sealer gets treated with a bonded acrylic made for non-porous surfaces —
          <strong>TravaCast</strong> — which guards against staining and
          discolouration on travertine, Spanish tile and stone coping. Which
          product your deck needs is exactly what the assessment decides.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">Pool deck questions</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Asked beside<br />every pool.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq rv">
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Will the deck be slippery afterwards?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Not with Salt Shield 303. It soaks into the concrete and dries clear
            with no film on top, and the manufacturer states plainly that it does
            not change the skid or slip resistance of the surface.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>My deck is already flaking. Is it too late?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Usually not — but the surface has to be corrected before any sealer
            goes down, because sealing over damage locks the damage in. That is a
            crack repair and restoration conversation first, and it is exactly what
            the free assessment is for.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>How long before the deck is back in use?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Twenty-four hours. Plan for one day around the pool rather than on it.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Does softener discharge really matter?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Yes — it is the same salt mechanism in a different spot. The slab your
            softener drains onto is quietly running the same experiment as your
            pool deck, so we check it while we are there.
          </p></div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Sub>
